Autonomous AI Agent Instruction Brief: LTX Video Model Distillation Pipeline

🎯 Objective

Your task is to distill a production-optimized video generation model tailored exclusively for Ken Burns-style motion on real estate interior/exterior images. The goal is to reduce generation time from 5–7 minutes per video down to 15–30 seconds, without sacrificing motion quality.

📦 What You're Working With

The user has an existing LTX Video model (likely LTX-Video by Lightricks) that currently handles general-purpose video generation. It is powerful but bloated for this specific task because it carries capacity for thousands of motion types the user will never need.

The user's actual workload is extremely narrow:

Input:** Static real estate photos (home interiors and exteriors)
Motion style:** Ken Burns — slow pan left-to-right, subtle zoom in, cinematic dolly push
Prompt template is almost always identical** with minor wording variations
Batch processing** — many images, same motion, over and over

This repetition is exactly what makes distillation so powerful here. You are not building a general model. You are building a specialist.

🧠 Core Concepts You Must Understand Before Acting

What is Distillation?
Distillation is the process of training a smaller, faster model to replicate the behavior of a larger, slower model — but only for a specific subset of tasks. The large model is called the teacher, the smaller one is the student. The student doesn't need to know everything the teacher knows — only what the user actually uses.

What is LoRA?
LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) is a fine-tuning technique that doesn't retrain the full model. Instead, it attaches small trainable adapter layers on top of the frozen base model. This means:
Significantly less compute needed
Far less training data required
The result is a small .safetensors or .pt file that modifies the base model's behavior at inference time
You can swap LoRA adapters in and out without changing the base model

What is Quantization?
Quantization reduces the numerical precision of the model's weights. By default, most models run in FP16 (16-bit floats). Quantizing to INT8 or INT4 means the model uses 8-bit or 4-bit integers instead, which dramatically reduces memory usage and speeds up inference on compatible hardware.

🔁 The Full Pipeline — Step by Step

Phase 1: Dataset Creation

Your first job is to build the training dataset. The user needs to generate their best 100–200 Ken Burns videos using their current full LTX model before any fine-tuning begins.

For each video, you must log and store:
The exact input image path
The exact prompt used
The output video file path
Any generation parameters used (steps, CFG scale, seed if relevant)

Store these as structured pairs — ideally a dataset.json or dataset.csv where each row is one {prompt, input_image, output_video} triplet. This is your ground-truth dataset. Quality matters more than quantity here — only include outputs the user considers visually good.

Minimum viable dataset: 50 pairs. Ideal: 100–200 pairs.

Phase 2: LoRA Fine-Tuning

Once the dataset exists, you will fine-tune the LTX model using LoRA. Here's what that means operationally:

You will use a LoRA training script compatible with LTX-Video. The most relevant open-source options are:
diffusers library by HuggingFace (has LoRA training support for video diffusion models)
kohya_ss trainer (widely used for LoRA on diffusion models)
Custom training scripts from the LTX-Video GitHub repo if they provide one

Key training configuration parameters the agent should set:
rank (LoRA rank): Start with r=16 or r=32 — higher rank = more expressive but slower
learning_rate: Typically 1e-4 to 5e-5 for video LoRA
epochs: 10–30 epochs on a dataset this size
batch_size: As large as VRAM allows (typically 1–2 for video)
target_modules: Attention layers (q_proj, v_proj, k_proj, out_proj)

The output of this phase is a LoRA checkpoint file — a small adapter (~50–500MB) that encodes the user's specific Ken Burns motion style.

Phase 3: Quantization

Critical rule: Quantize AFTER fine-tuning, never before. Quantizing before training degrades the gradients and produces a poor LoRA.

Once the LoRA is trained and validated, apply quantization to the base model + LoRA combination using one of these tools:
bitsandbytes library (easiest, integrates with diffusers)
llm.int8() or nf4 via HuggingFace transformers/diffusers load_in_4bit=True flags
GGUF format conversion for CPU/hybrid inference if needed

Target precision: INT4 (NF4) for maximum speed, or INT8 if the user notices quality degradation with INT4.

Expected outcome by method:

| Method | Speed Gain | Quality Impact |
|---|---|---|
| Base LTX (no changes) | 1x (baseline) | Full quality |
| INT8 quantization only | ~2x faster | Minimal loss |
| INT4 quantization only | ~3–4x faster | Minor loss |
| LoRA fine-tuned + INT4 | 5–10x faster | Preserved for narrow task |
| Smaller base model swap | Up to 10x faster | Moderate tradeoff |

Phase 4: Validation

Before deploying into the user's automation pipeline, run a validation pass:

Take 10 images NOT in the training set
Run them through the new distilled model (LoRA + INT4)
Compare output quality and motion style to the original full model outputs
Measure actual inference time
Report results back to the user with side-by-side comparisons

If motion quality is degraded, the agent should first try increasing LoRA rank or switching from INT4 to INT8 before any other changes.

Phase 5: Pipeline Integration

Once validated, the distilled model replaces the full LTX model in the user's batch processing workflow. The inference call should:

Load the base LTX model in INT4
Attach the trained LoRA adapter
Accept a folder of input images
Loop through each image, apply the standard Ken Burns prompt, save output video
Log completion with timestamps

⚠️ Key Constraints the Agent Must Respect

Never quantize before fine-tuning** — always fine-tune first, quantize second
Only use high-quality video outputs** for the training dataset — garbage in, garbage out
Do not change the prompt template** during dataset collection — consistency is what makes the LoRA work
Keep the original full model intact** — the LoRA is an addition, not a replacement of the base weights
Validate on unseen images** before declaring the pipeline production-ready

